<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<subsection xml:id="student_learning_outcomes" label="student_learning_outcomes" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Student Learning Outcomes</title>
  <p>
    Upon successful completion of this course, students will be able to
    <ul>
      <li>state the definitions and fundamental theorems concerning integral domains, field extensions, and Galois groups,</li>
      <li>determine whether a given ideal of a commutative ring is prime or maximal, and whether a given polynomial of low degree is irreducible,</li>
      <li>compute the degree of a field extension and the minimal polynomial of an algebraic element,</li>
      <li>describe the splitting field and the Galois group of a polynomial in concrete examples, and use the Fundamental Theorem of Galois Theory to determine the corresponding intermediate fields,</li>
      <li>write clear, logically correct proofs of statements in abstract algebra, and identify gaps or errors in a proposed proof.</li>
    </ul>
  </p>
  <p component="graduate">
    In addition, upon successful completion of this course, graduate students will be able to
    <ul>
      <li>construct and analyze examples beyond those treated in lecture, such as maximal ideals in polynomial rings in several variables and splitting fields presented as explicit quotients of polynomial rings,</li>
      <li>prove converses and generalizations of results from lecture, such as the fact that a polynomial ring <m>R[x]</m> is a principal ideal domain only when <m>R</m> is a field,</li>
      <li>determine the degrees and Galois groups of cyclotomic extensions, and use them to show that an extension of <m>\mathbb{Q}</m> generated by a linear combination of roots of unity is Galois with an abelian Galois group.</li>
    </ul>
  </p>
</subsection>
